WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.520
When one of your first jobs is sharing the&nbsp;
screen with the world's favorite web-slinger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.480
you're bound to be a star. Peyton List's&nbsp;
career has only gone up from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.215
and her transformation is truly stunning.

00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:16.240
List started her career as a child model but&nbsp;
transitioned to acting at a very early age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.360
That was seemingly always part of the plan,&nbsp;
with List admitting in a conversation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.800
Brooke Shields for Interview Magazine that she'd&nbsp;
always been eyeing a performing career. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.120
was thanks to her mother that the actor got&nbsp;
an opportunity to pursue her dream. She said,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.520
"I never thought of it as a career. It started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.360
off as an after-school thing I&nbsp;
would do growing up in New York."

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.240
List said that her twin brother, Spencer,&nbsp;
was approached first by local producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.960
and that her mother lobbied hard to&nbsp;
get her seen as well. She recalled,

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.960
"My mom said, 'Oh, well, my daughter is the&nbsp;
one who's always wanted to do it.' [...] And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.241
she kind of fought for me. So, my mom is&nbsp;
really the one that saw that I could do it."

00:00:50.241 --> 00:00:54.720
List would initially model in publications&nbsp;
like American Girl and Justice, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.880
appearing onscreen in several uncredited&nbsp;
roles. One of those uncredited gigs came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.720
in 2002 via a spot in the daytime soap opera "As&nbsp;
The World Turns." She'd go on to book roles in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.600
dozens of other film and television projects&nbsp;
before finally entering the Miyagi-verse.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.080
If you spend a few minutes scrolling through&nbsp;
Peyton List's filmography, you'll find a genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.800
impressive list of titles. But if you go back all&nbsp;
the way to the beginning of the actor's career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.840
you might be shocked to see List followed her&nbsp;
one-off spot on "As The World Turns" with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.819
brief role in one of the biggest blockbusters&nbsp;
of the early aughts: Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man 2."

00:01:27.819 --> 00:01:31.421
Yes, List's role in the film is&nbsp;
very much of the "uncredited" sort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:34.760
with the actor appearing alongside her&nbsp;
twin brother as a young girl chasing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.200
ball through the perilous streets of New York&nbsp;
City. The brief scene comes early in the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.720
when Peter Parker puts a make-or-break pizza&nbsp;
delivery on hold to save the young girl and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.640
her brother from being hit by a truck. In&nbsp;
terms of screen time, the scene is little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.400
more than a cameo for the baby-faced List&nbsp;
twins. But they do earn a line of dialogue.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.520
"Hey, you guys. No playing in the&nbsp;
streets." "Yes, Mr.Spider-man."

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.640
Brief as it is, the moment remains a memorable&nbsp;
one in what is arguably the best "Spider-Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.960
movie to date. And even at such a young age,&nbsp;
List's boundless charisma is well on display.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.320
Like many other young and upcoming actors, List&nbsp;
had the opportunity to play a younger version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:16.000
a big-name actor. And as it happens, she'd have&nbsp;
quite a bit of success booking such roles in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.000
first decade of her career, portraying the younger&nbsp;
version of some major stars over that period.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.680
One of the earliest of those roles found List&nbsp;
playing the pre-teen version of Katherine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.440
Heigl's Jane Nichols in the 2008 rom-com "27&nbsp;
Dresses." It remains arguably the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.400
of such roles for List, not only because "27&nbsp;
Dresses" was a massive hit at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.000
but because the young actor was front and&nbsp;
center as young Jane throughout the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.240
opening flashback. Two years later, she&nbsp;
appeared in "The Sorcerer's Apprentice,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.360
portraying young Becky Barns to her elder&nbsp;
on-screen counterpart, Teresa Palmer.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.160
She'd follow that gig by playing the child&nbsp;
version of Taryn Manning's Larissa Welsh in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.640
a 2011 episode of "Law &amp; Order: Special&nbsp;
Victims Unit." And that very same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:01.160
List turned up uncredited as the younger version&nbsp;
of Kate Hudson's Darcy in "Something Borrowed."

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.200
As it was, 2011 served as a bit of a breakthrough&nbsp;
year for Peyton List. In film, she appeared as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.240
Holly Hills in the beloved "Diary of a Wimpy Kid"&nbsp;
franchise. Adapted from the works of Jeff Kinney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:15.200
said franchise began in earnest the year&nbsp;
prior with the big-screen adaptation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.520
"Diary of a Wimpy Kid." The first&nbsp;
sequel — "Diary of a Wimpy Kid:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.840
Rodrick Rules" followed in 2011, with&nbsp;
List's kind-hearted Holly becoming a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.680
main player in the action after protagonist&nbsp;
Greg Heffley develops a major crush on her.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.080
Though Holly and Greg were on very friendly&nbsp;
terms by film's end, that crush goes largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.240
unrequited in "Rodrick Rules." Things, however,&nbsp;
heat up between the pair in the 2012 follow-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.560
"Diary of a Wimpy Kid: Dog Days." List&nbsp;
sees a major jump in screen time during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:45.400
the summer-set threequel, with Holly and&nbsp;
Greg becoming a full-blown couple by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.200
time the credits roll. In case there was&nbsp;
any question, their journey to coupledom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.680
is as hilarious as it is heartwarming. And&nbsp;
List is an absolute joy to watch throughout.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.360
Circling back to 2011, it turns out that was also&nbsp;
the year Peyton List landed what was then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.280
biggest role of her career, fronting the Disney&nbsp;
Channel hit "Jessie." List didn't book the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:09.400
role on the series, instead appearing as Emma&nbsp;
Ross, the somewhat ditzy and fashionable eldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:14.520
child of the family Jessie Prescott cares for.&nbsp;
As the actor said in an interview with PopSugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.400
although there were some differences between her&nbsp;
and the character she played on "Jessie," the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.960
share a love for fashion. List reprised her role&nbsp;
as Emma for the 2015 "Jessie" spin-off "Bunk'd"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.960
and also appeared in other Disney shows "Austin&nbsp;
&amp; Ally," "K.C. Undercover," and "I Didn't Do It."

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.400
Guest appearances aside, List played Emma for four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.080
full seasons of "Jessie." But&nbsp;
as big a break as the role was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.040
the actor admitted to Coveteur in 2017 that she&nbsp;
struggled with the fact that the general public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.120
tends to hold teen Disney stars to an almost&nbsp;
impossible real-world standard. She said,

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.640
"It's weird because Disney stars are normal&nbsp;
teenagers. Everyone expects that because they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.400
this show where everything is perfect — bad things&nbsp;
do happen, obviously — but I think they just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:00.840
expect them to be really conservative and always&nbsp;
cover up and not really be who they truly are."

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.560
"Look, I can't really chat right now because&nbsp;
I've got some holes in my memory to fill."

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:07.560
"Hey, well that's why I'm here. I can&nbsp;
totally help you fill your holes."

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.421
"Excuse me?"

00:05:08.421 --> 00:05:13.760
Perhaps in an effort to show who she really is,&nbsp;
after completing the 2016 Disney movie "The Swap,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.640
List put considerable effort into distancing&nbsp;
herself from her squeaky-clean Disney image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.560
During a 2021 chat with Interview Magazine, she&nbsp;
admitted that was tough in part because she'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:28.120
spent so much time making rounds with Disney&nbsp;
Channel fare, she'd forgotten how to audition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.560
and ultimately needed to relearn&nbsp;
how to keep calm during the process.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.760
Nevertheless, List found herself busy during&nbsp;
her post-Disney career, quickly landing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.920
lead in the independent film "Anthem of a&nbsp;
Teenage Prophet." The indie drama — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.920
co-starred "Shameless" breakout Cameron Monaghan&nbsp;
and "Yellowjackets" star Juliette Lewis — was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:49.280
heavier project that showcased her range&nbsp;
beyond family-friendly Disney offerings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.600
List has continued to surprise audiences since,&nbsp;
including with her role as Tory on "Cobra Kai."

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.840
In a funny twist, according to List's comments to&nbsp;
WWD, her Disney past helped her land the "Cobra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.360
Kai" role, as the creators of the Netflix hit&nbsp;
apparently couldn't pass up the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.280
to cast a former "Disney Princess" in&nbsp;
one of the series' most emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.360
complex roles. That gamble has officially&nbsp;
paid off big time for everyone involved.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:16.880
Though she's undoubtedly best known as an&nbsp;
actor at this point in her career, List&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.840
spent much of her pre-"Cobra Kai" time carving&nbsp;
out a secondary career in the music industry.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.360
While she'd been writing songs for years prior,&nbsp;
List waited until she was 20 to drop her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:31.760
single, 2018's breakup anthem "Liar Liar."&nbsp;
As she told Nylon in an interview coinciding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.360
with the release, she'd been holding off&nbsp;
recording the song for a while because,

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.680
"It took me a while to feel&nbsp;
super-confident and super-proud of it."

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.720
She said she also wanted to wait until her Disney&nbsp;
days were behind her before pivoting to music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.720
as she didn't want to be filed in with&nbsp;
every other Disney star-turned-pop singer.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:52.240
List was probably wise to wait, as she's been&nbsp;
able to work on her music completely on her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.880
terms and without Disney-sized expectations.&nbsp;
She has, however, only released a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:02.240
singles and music videos to date. And with no&nbsp;
new tracks since 2019, one can only assume her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.120
booming acting career is leaving little&nbsp;
time for the recording studio these days.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.160
Thanks to her role on "Cobra Kai," Peyton List is&nbsp;
a name well-respected in Hollywood circles today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.480
But even if her name now conjures images of a&nbsp;
talented child star transitioning to an adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.320
career, it's worth noting she's not the only&nbsp;
Peyton List in town. In fact, one could easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.920
argue the other Peyton List has had every bit&nbsp;
as compelling a career to date, with that older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.440
actor booking notable roles in "Mad Men," "The&nbsp;
Flash," "Gotham," and "The Rookie" over the years.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.720
It's pretty rare that two actors&nbsp;
forge ahead with the exact same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:39.240
as SAG-AFTRA rules tend to limit such issues.&nbsp;
It's even rarer that two actors with the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.440
name have the sort of success the&nbsp;
Peyton Lists have had. And yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:47.000
it's almost unheard of that the two might end up&nbsp;
on the same call sheet for any film or television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:52.360
project. Yet that's precisely what happened&nbsp;
in a 2002 episode of "As The World Turns," in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:58.000
which the two Peytons apparently appeared not&nbsp;
just in the same episode, but the same scene.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.120
The two Peyton Lists joined the screen&nbsp;
again together when both actors were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.520
credited for voice performances in the&nbsp;
2019 animated superhero hit "Batman: Hush."

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:08.980
"Take care of your boss."

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:12.360
"Ugh, he's not my boss!"

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:16.240
Sadly, the two do not share any scenes in&nbsp;
"Hush," with the elder List giving voice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.720
the nefarious Poison Ivy and the younger voicing&nbsp;
the butt-kicking Barbara Gordon, aka Batgirl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:27.600
The film was adapted from a popular 2002 story arc&nbsp;
from DC Comics that found the titular baddie using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.840
his shocking wealth of knowledge about Batman&nbsp;
to wreak havoc on the man behind the mask and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.960
his allies. "Hush" debuted at the 2019 San Diego&nbsp;
Comic-Con the day before its streaming release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.680
and remains one of the best-loved of&nbsp;
the recent "Batman" animated features.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.520
As for the younger List, she obviously is not&nbsp;
fronting the action here. But her Batgirl does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.880
play a pivotal role in the overarching narrative.&nbsp;
And given how strong List is in the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:53.760
one has to wonder why she's done so&nbsp;
little voice work in the years since.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.440
List booked her "Cobra Kai" role the year before&nbsp;
"Batman: Hush" was released. But given that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:02.480
Season 2 debut as Tory Nichols came when&nbsp;
the series was still part of the YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:07.200
Premium slate of original programming, it's&nbsp;
not entirely shocking List landed the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.600
as she'd already starred in a pair of original&nbsp;
films for the then-developing streaming service.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:17.560
Those films were 2016's "The Thinning," and its&nbsp;
2018 sequel "The Thinning: New World Order."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.680
The young adult dramas are a sort of hybrid&nbsp;
between "The Hunger Games" and "The Purge,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.280
unfolding in a not-too-distant future in which&nbsp;
overcrowding has led society to take extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:31.200
measures in population control. In this case,&nbsp;
that means straight-up executing any and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.920
student who fails to pass a certain standardized&nbsp;
test. The films find List sharing the screen with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:40.840
the controversy-courting Logan Paul, with their&nbsp;
characters discovering unsettling discrepancies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:46.360
in the fateful test and eventually uncovering the&nbsp;
ghastly government conspiracy behind the process.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.320
"The Thinning" films are hardly aspiring&nbsp;
to high art. But the audience scores for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.880
both the original, and its sequel&nbsp;
are decent enough. So they might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.440
worth a look if you're hot to do a deep&nbsp;
dive on List's pre-"Cobra Kai" career.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.080
Then came "Cobra Kai." The role of&nbsp;
Tory Nichols has given Peyton List an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:06.680
opportunity to showcase her star power.&nbsp;
As the actor told Screen Rant in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:11.280
she continues to have a lot of fun with the&nbsp;
character in what she deems a dream job. She said,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.560
"It is the most fun role I've ever had the&nbsp;
chance to play. And every day I'm just like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:19.200
how did I get this? And how am&nbsp;
I here? I love this so much."

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.440
That joy clearly comes across as&nbsp;
List embodies every facet of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:25.760
character's physical and emotional&nbsp;
journey with disarming grace.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.080
Playing Tory has also given List an opportunity&nbsp;
to develop legit martial arts skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.640
which she reportedly refines during workouts&nbsp;
at a small, private dojo owned by the uncle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.040
a friend. The actor has said as well that&nbsp;
she finds her "Cobra Kai" role empowering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.800
as it forces her to train so heavily. And any&nbsp;
"Cobra Kai" fan can attest that List puts those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.960
skills to extraordinary use during scenes&nbsp;
that require her to speak with her fists.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:51.480
"Come on. My sister always promised to&nbsp;
take care of me. She taught you better."

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.640
"Yeah, she taught me to&nbsp;
recognize a low-class leech."

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.040
But the actor is arguably at her best on "Cobra&nbsp;
Kai" when she's speaking with her voice. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.800
considering her origins as a child model and&nbsp;
Disney Channel star, List's transformation into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.855
fully-developed karate antagonist has proven quite&nbsp;
an unexpected, if most welcome surprise indeed.

